let __v_63 = Value::from("Keep a dummy for sleep until about 12 months, because it lowers the risk of cot death, then take it away in the day first and leave it for naps and nights only. Between one and two is the easiest window - after two and a half the child can argue for it. Pick a quiet week, expect two or three rough nights, and never dip it in sugar or honey or hang it on a string round the neck.");
frame.store("_DUMMY_BABY", __v_63.clone())?;
let __v_64 = Value::from("At %s a dummy is a habit rather than a need, so plan it rather than spring it: say \"dummies are for sleep now\" and take it away in the day first, then the nap one, then the night one, a step a week. A %s year old can be in on the plan, so pick the week together, and get the dummies out of the house rather than into a drawer. Expect a few rough nights and do not give it back mid-week: going back and forth is what makes it last.");
frame.store("_DUMMY_HABIT", __v_64.clone())?;
let __v_65 = Value::from("Past five a dummy is only worth acting on if it is pushing the teeth or the speech out of line, and that is a dentist's or a speech therapist's call rather than something to fix at home. Do not shame the child or turn it into a fight: say it is time, give the date, and most children drop it themselves once it is not a secret. If the teeth are moving or the speech is unclear, ask the dentist at the next check-up and say how often it is used.");
